<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Syrian Coastal Mountain Range</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jabal al-Nusayriyah, Jabal Ansariyah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Syrian Coastal Mountain Range runs north to south parallel to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, between the coastal plain and the Orontes valley, rising to about 1,500 m. Known in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> as the Jabal Bahra and later as the Jabal al-Nusayriyah or Jabal Ansariyah, it sheltered religious minorities: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismaili-state">Nizari Ismailis</a> held castles there from the 12th century, and it is the historic heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alawites">Alawites</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/syrian-coastal-mountain-range.jpg" alt="Syrian Coastal Mountain Range" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CIA; derivative work by Supreme Deliciousness · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Syria_2004_CIA_map-2010-07-09.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The range is on average 32 kilometres wide, with peaks averaging just over 1,200 metres; the highest, Nabi Yunis, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latakia">Latakia</a>, reaches 1,562 metres. Heights fall to about 900 metres in the north and 600 metres in the south. The western slopes catch moist winds from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and are more fertile and more densely settled than the eastern side.</p><p>On the east the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontes-river">Orontes River</a> flows north through the Ghab Plain, a trench 64 kilometres long, then turns round the northern end of the range to reach the sea. South of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masyaf">Masyaf</a> a large strike-slip fault separates the range from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> and the Anti-Lebanon Mountains, forming the Homs Gap.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In classical times the range was called the Bargylus, a name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> records. It probably came from Barga, an ancient city-kingdom near the mountains that belonged to the Eblaite Empire in the third millennium BCE and was later a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>, who named the range after it. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> the mountains took the name Jabal Bahra from the Arab tribe of Bahra', and they have been home to an Alawite population since the Middle Ages.</p><p>Their other names, the Nusayriyah or Ansarieh Mountains and the Alawiyin Mountains, all refer to the Alawi community; the first rests on an old label for it that is now considered insulting. Between 1920 and 1936 the mountains formed part of the eastern border of the Alawite State within the French Mandate for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Syrian Coastal Mountain Range” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1132 they acquired castles in the <b>Jabal Bahra</b>, the coastal mountains of Syria, including Qadmus, Kahf and in 1140-1141 Masyaf.</blockquote><a href="/state/nizari-ismailis-of-syria">Nizari Ismailis of Syria</a></li></ul></section>
